1 NAME Birger /Magnus/
2 GIVN Birger
2 SURN Magnus
2 _MARNM Magnus
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Øvsttun Kirkegård
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bergen
3 STAE Hordaland
3 CTRY Norway
1 BIRT
2 DATE 30 MAR 1900
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bryne
3 STAE Rogaland
3 CTRY Norway
1 DEAT
2 DATE 6 NOV 1970
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bergen
3 STAE Hordaland
3 CTRY Norway
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Birger Magnus grew up in Nubbebakken in Bergen and at his parents country home in Skjold, Bergen. He was 15 years old when he moved to "Hasselbakken"  at Øvre Hopsnesveien in Bergen. He took his middle school exams
2 CONC  at Hartmann Private school in Oslo and thereafter got his high school degree from Bergen Handelsgymnasium. According to his wife, Sigrid, he was a happy and pleasant boy.  When he lived at Hop as a boy, where the family had a sma
2 CONC ll farm, he had a riding horse. When he was in the military (which was obligatory in those days) at Ulven camp his Captain got to borrow his horse.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was never Hans K. Magnus intention that Birger would take over the factory so Birger started his work in Bergen Kreditbank (later merged with Bergen Bank which was eventually merged with DnB) and thereafter traveled to Boston, 
2 CONC USA to practice business and study.  However, he was soon called back to Bergen and was soon thereafter sent to Germany where he received practical education in one of the lace machine construction factories whom did business with
2 CONC  the family company Magnus and Co. He was quite good with languages and spoke very well German and English. He though, himself, that the stay in Germany was of little use as he spent most of his time filing machine parts. He made 
2 CONC many friends in Germany which he kept in touch with until his last days.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contemporary people in the same business told that he worked hard in the factory as well when he was out visiting customers. He could take the night train from Bergen to Oslo, visit customers all day there and the following night 
2 CONC take the night train to Trondheim and visit more customers there, then night train back to Oslo for more visits and finally night train back home to Bergen.  The 1920 and 1930s were difficult times for the business. The lace was b
2 CONC rought home to be "pulled" and Birger satt many evenings and set up lace at the factory.  He told dramatically about the time in 1931 when his father came and told him that Bergen Privatbank had stopped making payments for the fac
2 CONC tory. Birger wanted to expand the the production into women's laced underwear but his father Hans Krohn Magnus would not dare to spend the money. Hans Magnus died suddenly in 1935 from a heart attach 63 years old. Birger did not h
2 CONC esitate to order new lace machines in Germany. From this the Women's laced underwear brand "Ballet" was born.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans K. Magnus had been the owner (disponent) of Bø Teglverk AS (Bø Brick Factory, see Christian Magnus) after his father Christian L. Magnus. Bergen Privatbank had been the  main shareholder with Christopher Magnus (Birger's Uncl
2 CONC e) as the co owner and the company had gone bankrupt. Times were better now and Birger was in 1936 offered to buy the company for NOK60,000 with NOK5,000 as cash and NOK55,000 as a loan from the same bank. Bø Teglverk became very 
2 CONC dear to Birger and became a very good business for him for many years. He also built a small house next to the factory which was located at Karmøy, Rogaland that he and his family used to stay in when he visited the factory.
2 CONT
2 CONT Birger and Sigrid was married in 1923 in Bergen and first lived in Zetlits Gate and then in Welhavens gate in Bergen. They later moved to Nesttun, Bergen in 1928 and later built the house called Veslehaugen at Øvre Hopsnesveien 9?
2 CONC  right next to his parents house, Hasselbakken, in 1929. The house cost NOK28,000. The raw materials was harder to get a hold of during the WW2 years in 1940 to 1945. The lace factory stopped completely but  a little underwear was
2 CONC  being made and some was taken in for repair. Birger thought that WW2 had taken the best years of his life. The family did not suffer in any significant way during the war but there was little activity in the lace factory. The Ger
2 CONC mans that were occupying the country during the war dearly needed the building material from the brick factory and Birger thought, like most others, that it was better that the business went to a Norwegian company then for the fac
2 CONC tory to be confiscated by the Germans. The Brick factory ran at full output all through the second world war.  
2 CONT
2 CONT At the lace factory there was Manager with the name Meister. He ran a pub in the evening for German soldiers in the old Prayer House at Nesttun, Bergen. After a while he started threatening Birger that he would take over the facto
2 CONC ry all on his own.  Birger then went to the German Government in Norway to find out how he should act on such a threat. Birger spoke perfect German and the end of the story was that Mr. Meister had to leave the factory. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war the raw materials was again available but production equipment was still rationed. The demand was very high but it was very difficult to get permits to expand. The socialist labour party "Arbeider partiet" took power
2 CONC  in the country and Birger thought that the Norwegian state would soon take everything and could not find any reason to expand. He, whom had in his younger years, been very expansion driven, became now passive. That was possible a
2 CONC  good thing for the company. Many companies expanded heavily after the war and later got into trouble when the competition became harder due to the import limitations being lifted in 1951 when cheaper imports would compete with No
2 CONC rwegian manufactured good.
2 CONT
2 CONT Birger was good at delegating and he was easy to cooperate with in the company. He was a friendly and gentle man both privately and at work and he treated all people nicely. He  was known to receive sale people well. Some sales pe
2 CONC ople still remember him for that. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The synthetic fiber created new possibilities for the lace factory and new expansion possibilities. Birger controlled the brick factory and the lace factory. The laced underwear business was left to his son Hans Petter Magnus.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1965 he got a small brain blood clot and he was partially paralyzed in one side. He felt that he was hanging in a thin thread but he was still going into the office and looked after all of the departments until he died in 1970.
2 CONC  He was at that time employing about 160 people. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Birger told that he was brought up very strictly by his father. He wanted to be different than his father in bringing up his own children. The children of Birger remember their father, Birger, as kind and doting father. It was mos
2 CONC tly his wife Sigrid that had to be strict with the children. Birger's hobby was fishing and reading newspaper. He was only a bit interested in gardening but liked to specialize on the walk ways in the gardens. When he was young he
2 CONC  did not mind working hard but as an older man he was physically lazy (according to his daughter Kerstin and his wife Sigrid). One time he also went on a camping trip. He was a decent skier. His son, Hans Petter, remembers taking 
2 CONC the train to Trengereid (along the train track from Bergen to Oslo) and walked back home over Gulfjellet, Austlifjellet, Katlane and Livarden. This was prior to the war. He did the trip for his last time and his son Hans Petter di
2 CONC d the trip for his first time. Birger skied until he was about 64 years old. He was healthy and not sick often.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Birger's death the business was split between the three siblings. Hans Petter got the ownership of the laze factory at Nesttun and the two sisters ended up with the ownership of Bø Teglverk AS. Magnus and Co are mostly doing
2 CONC  property investments in Bergen and Bø Teglverk does mostly property investments as well. Hans Petter ended up owning the large house Hasselbakken and his two sisters inherited properties at Hopsneset where Birger's daughter Kerst
2 CONC in M. Magnus (married Hvide) built a house at Øvrehopsnesveien 18 in 1959 and at Øvrehopsnesveien 14 in 1974. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His grandchildren whom lived next door to Birger and Sigrid Magnus;  Hans Petter's children Birger (Bie) and Anniken Magnus and the five children of Kerstin and Wollert Hvide remember him smoking his pipe and sitting on his lap in
2 CONC  his living room. When the children came to visit their grandparent they always got ice cream that was retrieved from the freezer in the basement by Birger's wife Sigrid.
2 CONT
2 CONT He talked about him self as being a bit lazy and this probably limited his ambitions.
