1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *
2 CONT https://www.gravsted.dk/person.php?navn=varinkamuus
2 CONT **
2 CONT
2 CONT ONE of the last key figures in the Second World War Danish Resistance, Varinka Wichfeld-Muus was born into an aristocratic provincial milieu on the island of Lolland, south Denmark, closely involved in the struggle against the occ
2 CONC upying German forces.
2 CONT
2 CONT While her father, Jørgen Wichfeld, assumed a more or less neutral stance against the Nazi invaders, Varinka’s Anglo-Irish aristocrat mother, Monica Wichfeld (née Massy-Beresford), worked closely with Free Denmark and Britain’s Spe
2 CONC cial Operations Executive (SOE) in sabotage activities before being arrested by the Germans in 1944 and sentenced to death for refusing to leak information about her involvement in the Resistance and her contacts. Monica Wichfeld’
2 CONC s sentence was commuted to life imprisonment, but she died of tuberculosis in prison in Waldheim, Germany, in February 1945, aged only 50.
2 CONT
2 CONT Varinka had helped her mother with arms pickups, hiding and feeding wanted persons, running errands and becoming a resistance leader in her own right, before joining the legendary Flemming Muus as secretary — and wife after a secr
2 CONC et wedding — in Copenhagen in 1944 at the height of the Danish resistance effort against Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Flemming Muus, who died in 1982 aged 74, was at that time SOE’s chief agent in Denmark and an Allied Observer on the Freedom Council, the Danish resistance body. More a personal assistant than a secretary, Varinka spent much of he
2 CONC r time and energy at the tail end of the war and in the ensuing years defending her husband against allegations of embezzling SOE and other funds entrusted to him in his capacity of Danish resistance leader.
2 CONT
2 CONT In an atmosphere of internal intrigue and infighting among rowing resistance factions at the end of the occupation, Muus was arrested in June 1946 and sentenced by a Copenhagen court to two years imprisoment for embezzlement, a se
2 CONC ntence that was set aside on appeal, on condition that he went into voluntary exile from Denmark for five years.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1946 to 1949 Muus and Varinka travelled in luxury to England, South Africa (where he became director of a goldmining company for a while) and Italy, before returning home to Denmark. After Muus’s death, Varinka, a fiery, rest
2 CONC ive woman, involved herself in virulent attacks against those responsible for her late husband’s imprisonment, exile and disgrace.
2 CONT
2 CONT Muus himself admitted that large sums of money passed through his hands and that he was slipshod at accounts, but emphasised that security reasons demanded fiscal secrecy and denied all embezzlement charges. The British had only p
2 CONC raise for his Second World War activities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Varinka’s memoirs Fra Solskin til Tusmoerke (From Sunshine to Twilight) were published in 1994.
2 CONT
2 CONT Varinka Wichfeld-Muus was born in Saxkøbing, Denmark, on February 9, 1922. She died of cancer in Copenhagen on December 18, 2002, aged 80.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Source==
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.timesonline.co.uk/tol/comment/obituaries/article809920.ece The Times]
2 CONT
