1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Translated from [http://www.mosshistorielag.no/artikler-samla-fra-strandsittaren/474-carl-ludvig-stabell Moss historielag]:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Carl Ludvig Stabell''' came from a large family. In a book about the Stabell family that a major Castberg wrote in 1955, it is stated that the historically certain portion of the Stabell family gets its beginnings around 1600. 
2 CONC But he points to a Danish historian who believes he can prove that the family comes from the region of Schleswig where it was mentioned already in 1259.
2 CONT
2 CONT An ancestor, Christen Bastiansøn Stabell, was councilman in Trondheim in 1640 to the 1650's. The family was partly merchants in Trondheim, partly officers or self-owning farmers in Trøndelag. Carl Ludvig's father was called Paul U
2 CONC lrik Stabell and was born in Sparbu 27 January 1805. He was married in 1834 with Marianne Jørgine Dagenbolt, the daughter of a colonel. Paul Stabell was cand. theol. from 1831. He worked several years as a teacher - came in 1838 t
2 CONC o Molde as a teacher in public school. In 1843 he became vicar of Edøy, then a separate municipality - today part of Smøla municipality. These islands are located northwest of Kristiansund.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Edøy Paul Stabell entered directly into the affairs of local government, as it says in the family book. He was mayor from 1846 to 1849 and from 1856 to 1859. He, or rather his wife, ran the parsonage with great vigor. It was sa
2 CONC id that there were 8 maids and 6 servant boys on the farm. They also held a fishing boat fully staffed in summer and winter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul Stabell had 9 children - Carl Ludvig was no. 4, born 9 October 1840 at Fanestranden near Molde in Romsdal county. He got an engineering degree at Polytechnikum in Karlsruhe. Then training as a reserve officer for possible Nor
2 CONC wegian participation in the German war in 1864.
2 CONT
2 CONT After a brief employment in ''Statens veivesen'' (the National Road Administration), he came to Moss as manager of ''Moss Gassverk'' (the Moss gas works) in 1866. The gas plant was established in 1857 on a plot of Værlesanden, add
2 CONC ress Stengata 2. Stabell stayed at the gas works for the rest of his life. He was unmarried. ''Byfogd'' (town judge) Sandberg writes in 1896 in his book "Kjøbstaden Moss" ("The market town Moss"):
2 CONT
2 CONT On 30 March 1875 it was decided to establish a position as borough engineer. Stabell was employed as the very first one. There were no specific instructions, but Sandberg writes: His most significant functions are: Be fire chief. 
2 CONC Manager of the water works (finished in 1876). Be manager of the gas company. Be warden of the town's road and sewage administration. Carry out all work on the canal and the port. Undertake any work concerning the municipality. He
2 CONC  was for instance involved in the erection of all school buildings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later - 17 July 1887 - he was appointed as borough conductor by the county of Smaalenene. The reason was that the municipality had introduced compulsory surveying of plots.
2 CONT
2 CONT Already in 1845 a general building law had been adopted which required a street width of 12 metros, but even after the town fire in 1858 such wide streets were not laid. A little later it was decided that streets that were 10 m wi
2 CONC de should have a carriageway of 6m and two pavements, each of 2 m.
2 CONT
2 CONT A commission was responsible for the regulation. It consisted of the magistrate, police chief and borough engineer. Before 1882 the street names were a result of tradition. But in 1882 the council's executive board conducted a nam
2 CONC ing ceremony for the streets - according to the Danish town history Stabell was the driving force.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of significant projects that Stabell led in the late 1800s were, among others, new canal bridge, ''Middelskolen'' (middle school), Moss folkeskole (elementary school) and Pikeskolen (girls' schoal). He retired as manager of the ga
2 CONC s works in 1909, but continued as borough engineer to 1916. Then he had been employed by the municipality for 50 years including 40 as borough engineer. Soon after he received a blow over the foot, got gangrene and had to amputate
2 CONC . After that it went down quickly and he died 2 September 1917.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the obituaries in the Moss newspapers stood among others: Every year, an increasing number of objectives and tasks were assigned to Stabell. He received them without thinking it might be different. He performed them all calmly 
2 CONC and conscientiously. Work was his life - he knew no normal working hours or holidays. And with utter calm he received the complaints that were his and all borough engineers' solder to get. But he could also bite down if the attack
2 CONC s got personal or were unfair on the municipality.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stabell was considered aloof and withdrawn. But at the same time he was also generous and caring. For example, when his younger brother died in 1894 only 40 years old, Carl Ludvig took care of the widow and the two youngest childr
2 CONC en. They stayed with Stabell in Moss until his death in 1917. The youngest, Leif Westad Stabell, was sent to Bergen where he graduated from the Bergen Technical School in 1914.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leif Westad Stabell was briefly an assistant engineer in Moss municipality, and after short periods in Tønsberg and Haugesund, he was in 1918 employed in Halden, where he was the rest of his working life. He was building manager t
2 CONC here in the 1960's.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Carl Ludvig resigned as borough engineer in 1916, he was succeeded by a man named Ystgaard.
2 CONT
