1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Kerstin vokste opp på Hopseneset i huset som er kalt Hasselbakken. Hun bodde på Hop hele sitt liv. Først bygget de et lite hus på tomten av faren hennes på Øvrehopsnesveien 18. Senere, i 1975 bygget de et hus på Øv
2 CONC rehopsnesveien 14, som var meget større men var også rett ved siden, Hasselbakken,  der hun vokste opp. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Kerstin misted sin første sønn i en druknings ulykke da han var 2 år gammel. Døds ulykken skyltes at en hushjelp ikke passet på barnet og barnet gikk alene ned til Nordås vannet. Kerstin hadde sett tilfeller tidligere at hushjelpe
2 CONC n ikke passet på og Kerstin mente selv at hun skulle ha visst om at hushjelpen ikke var pålitelig. Kerstin fortalte oss at det var ikke en dag hun ikke tenkte på dette. Kersting bebreidet seg selv for denne ulykken og tilga seg se
2 CONC lv ikke før de siste par årene hun levde. Kerstin døde den 15de November og hennes eldste sønn døde den 14de November 60 år tidligere.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Friday 30th November the funeral was held at Storetveit kirke at 12 noon. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the service in the church,  a reception was held at the Reksten Samlingene in Bergen starting at 2pm. After the reception,  there was a big dancing party with a popular band at the same place that lasted to midnight. The
2 CONC re were at least 200 people present, friends and family. The party was an instruction from Kerstin that after she died there should be a big party. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This was Anders Hvide's speech during the funeral church sermon (the speech was originally given in Norwegian):  '' ''''Dear Mom,  I've been dreading to give this speech. In fact, for several years - for I knew that the day once n
2 CONC aturally would come.  You were born in 1931 at Hop, the youngest of three children. You were the spoiled little girl who was sitting on Grandfather's lap when you wanted something. With two pretty much older siblings, you probably
2 CONC  got enough attention from your parents.  Primary school and secondary school at Fana, then Bergens Handelsgym. There you met your life's love - our father. In 1954 there was a wedding at Hop. You became Mrs. Hvide and would care 
2 CONC for the father, but you didn’t know how to cook – as a child your family had a cook at home at “Hasselbakken». When you tried to boil potatoes without water in the casserole, you probably realized that something was wrong.  The fi
2 CONC rst child came in 1956 - Wollert Andreas.  The tragedy happened when he drowned 2 years later. Of course, this was catastrophic for you, and we children never understood the effect this had on you and Dad. You told me that you nev
2 CONC er managed to cry properly after our brother's passing away - you were in shock.  The way for you and your father to get over the tragedy was to have many children. We were born on a string; in '59, '61, '63, '65 and '67.  You kep
2 CONC t the house in order while Dad was at work and tried to build up his own shipping company. It is admirable that you managed this mother. Admittedly, you had some help at home, but more or less the responsibility was only on you.  
2 CONC You were a faithful wife and mother. You were what we needed! You even learned to sail even if Dad had to teach you again every spring when the sailing season started. 
2 CONT
2 CONT BREAK  You were our dear mother - loving with long and warm hugs. You lived for us children and Dad. That was what was most important to you. You used to say that you were the richest in the neighborhood because you had five child
2 CONC ren!  You had a lot of empathy, you treated everyone alike.  Everyone was welcome home to us. At times the whole street was with us and played. You were really our local kindergarden «aunt». We remember well that you always baked 
2 CONC wheat rolls on Saturdays, which of course was very popular among the kids on our street.  I also remember when I was 17 years old and came home and found you in the living room with four French ladies in their 20's. This is very d
2 CONC escriptive of your personality Mom. The French girls had gotten lost on their way to Edvard Grieg’s “Troldhaugen». You not only explained the direction in French - you invited them to erect their tent in our garden. Not only that 
2 CONC - you also invited them to dinner.  You were fair and sometimes a little too direct. You were not always as diplomatic. From you, people got to hear the truth.  I remember you remodeled Wollert and Brit's living room while they we
2 CONC re at work. You also began to talk about my ex-girlfriend during Cathrine's first dinner at Hop. Yes, yes, it was you in a nutshell, but we knew everything you said was in the best meaning.  You were a consistently good person who
2 CONC m your friends and family appreciated. Loyal and supportive when needed.  You were also a person that could live with high risk – in fact you were fearless.  It's amazing what you allowed us to do. You sent us alone to the USA as 
2 CONC 16-year olds, even though we had hardly been abroad before. The two elders went on a world circumnavigation trip with our sailboat. You let us work at sea as 12-year-olds.  Our last trip together was to Paris. Only you and me. You
2 CONC  chirped like a young girl on the plane. You were an au pair in Paris in the early 1950's and you would like to experience Paris again. Several times during the weekend did you receive compliments from strangers for your good Fren
2 CONC ch. You had a linguistic ear mother - fluent in English, French and German.  Dad’s death 13 years ago you took very heavily. Your soulmate was gone - he who gave you praise and reproach - he you shared joy and concerns with. Your 
2 CONC longing for dad was hard for you during your last years. We all noticed that. You were really a bit fed up with life.  You didn’t want to move into an apartment - you wanted to be carried out of the house.  Born at Hop, lived at H
2 CONC op, and passed away at Hop.  Your passing came as a shock to us, but we are grateful that you didn’t feel any pain.  Five children, 17 grandchildren and 6 great-grandchildren will remember you with warmth and love.  Thank you for 
2 CONC everything you have been to us.  We will never forget you. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rest in peace mom.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The below speech was made by the priest during the funeral church sermon:
2 CONT
2 CONT Memory word Kerstin Margrethe Hvide Kerstin Margrethe Hvide was born on September 24, 1931, as daughter of Birger and Sigrid Magnus. Along with the siblings Elna and Hans Petter, she grew up in the house called Hasselbakken on Hop
2 CONC sneset. She had a good childhood, like the family's dear and spoiled youngest girl. Already at the beginning of her teens, she met the love of her life, Wollert August Hvide. On the question of how it happened they became two, Ker
2 CONC stin could answer dry: "He had no chance. He was mine. "After completing her high  school, she traveled a year on a language trip to France. Then she worked as a secretary in Bergen and New York while Wollert studied his shipping 
2 CONC school. In 1955 the wedding was held, and the party was held on Hasselbakken. A year later Kerstin gave birth to his first son, named after Wollert. But when the boy was only 2 and a half years he died tragically in a drowning acc
2 CONC ident, a mourning as she herself expressed it: "you can never get over it , but you can learn to live with it." Since then Kerstin gave birth to five children in eight years. It was Johan Gustav, Wollert, Anders, Hans Jacob and Ke
2 CONC rstin Birgitte. It was a busy life. Wollert worked hard as a foundation, while Kerstin had a full time job as a house wife. The children think of her of a warm, brave and willing mother. For Kerstin took a choice: Although she had
2 CONC  lost a child in an accident, she was careful not to give the children an over protected upbringing. They did not like what had happened. In the summer the family traveled to southern Norway. While Wollert transported the family y
2 CONC acht from Bergen, Kerstin filled the car with children, sat down behind the wheel and drove out on the long ride on the gravel roads over the mountains. Kerstin was a strong and independent lady. If a letter addressed to "Mrs. Shi
2 CONC p Owner Wollert Hvide" appeared in the mailbox, it was sent in return. Unknown addressee!!. Not because she did not live close to her husband. The two were inseparable. But she was so much more than just a shipowners wife. She als
2 CONC o showed this in her intense energy and her unobtrusive lifestyle. Kerstin wanted the home on Hopsneset to be open and generous. She had a strong social conscience and tried so well she could do good to others. Therefore it was a 
2 CONC pleasure for her to have a wedding party on Hasselbakken when a good friend who also worked as a maid for the family got married. It also brought the family who grew up with in-law children, grandsons and grandchildren also notice
2 CONC . Kerstin was proud of the family and never forgot when the in-law child or one of the 17 grandchildren filled years. Kerstin was distinctly social. She was in the bridge club, cultivating her interest in classical music in harmon
2 CONC y every Thursday. Kerstin and Wollert had worked hard for a long time and looked forward to enjoying retirement together. But in 2000, Wollert became seriously ill. It was a heavy period for Kerstin. For five years, she took care 
2 CONC of him with devoted care, and when he left us in 2005, sadness was intense ad she was a bit lost The pain of losing her life's partner and best friend was great. Even Kerstin was a good lady all the way to the end. She went for lo
2 CONC ng walks and was out with family and friends daily. But on the 15th of November, the body said  stop, she was 87. She had lived a long and good life. We are delighted with Kerstin Margrethe Hvide's memory.
2 CONT
2 CONT The below speech was given by Kerstin's daughter Kerstin Birgitte Merzoug during the funeral.
2 CONT
2 CONT Det sies at bak en hver suksessrik mann stär en sterk kvinne. Mor var denne kvinnen, hun var sterk og hun stod ved far side og stöttet og korrigerte ham i alt han gjorde. Mor mätte ikke sträle i kapp med far, men hadde en egen sik
2 CONC kerhet som gjorde at hun fungerte fint ved siden av ham eller alene.
2 CONT
2 CONT Det er vanskelig ä snakke om mor uten ä ta med far. De var et godt team. Mor og far ble tidlig et team, og traff  hverandre allerede pä Handelsgym. Mor hadde ikke store ambisjoner pä skolebenken, men hun var nysgjerrig og etter ar
2 CONC tium reiste hun til England, Frankrike og Tyskland for ä laere spräk og for komme seg ut i verden. Barnepass og det som fulgte med ved ä vaere aupair var den gangen av mindre  interesse……………… Hun var  som ung overhodet ikke intere
2 CONC ssert i hus og hjem, og jeg tror nok at far tenkte sitt da mor som nygift ville lage brunsaus. Hun tok da sukkerkulör oppi den hvite sausen, og den ble brun. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Men mor var en klok kvinne, og hun skjönte fort at  dette noe hun mätte  laere.  - og det gjorde hun. Vi  kunne ikke klage og det  var vi tok det nesten for gitt da  mor serverte nybakte boller midtfjors pä seiltur  oppi Lofoten. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Egentlig var mor  lat av natur, men med sä mange barn kunne hun ikke ligge pä latsiden. Det var bare til frokost i helgene hun fikk lov ä vaere lat. Da ble hun ikke sulten, helt til frokosten stod pä bordet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mor var en god i ä organisere og ordnet opp i alt hjemme. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Det var litt av en logestikk när vi skulle reise pä fjellet med fem barn, hund og katt, mormor, julestek og skiutstyr, - og av og til gikk det galt. Det var ikke alltid vi fikk med oss alt, feks sekken med skiskoene, julesteken el
2 CONC lerden gangen vi glemte mormor. Da gikk det gjetord i juletoget til Ustaoset  at Hvidene hadde glemt mormoren sin. Ja, jeg ble ogsä engang glemt en gang, stäende i grindsengen, men det ble heldigvis oppdaget nede i svingen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Slike ting ville kanskje noen synes var pinlig, eller man skulle helst ikke snakke om det, men slik var ikke mor. Hun forstod hva som var viktigst i livet og brydde seg ikke sä mye om hva andre folk sa. Hun var ärligheten selv. Ja
2 CONC , av og til tider for aerlig, og det var da hun av og til träkket i salaten. Som da en studievenninne av meg som er 1.59 höy reiste seg fra stolen sin for ä  hilse pä min mor for förste gang, og mor utbröt: äh, jeg visste du var l
2 CONC iten, men ikke sä liten!  i tillegg til ä vaere flink ä organisere var hun ogsä  veldig praktisk og det var ikke alltid at vi var enige i hennes lösninger. Jeg tror jeg var den eneste komfirmanten som hadde komfirmasjonbesöket med
2 CONC  hele familien pä lördagen og komfimerte meg pä söndagen. Det var nemlig bare lördagen kokken kunne komme.
2 CONT
2 CONT Men mor laerte oss ä ha respekt for andre, og ikke minst ä ha respekt for hverandre. I en ungeflokk pä fem er det mange meninger og vi laerte ä ta hensyn til hverandre og ta vare pä hverandre. Hun tok ogsä hensyn til menneskene ru
2 CONC ndt seg, og viste like stor respekt uansett posisjon og tittel.  Huset värt var alltid äpent for gjester og for väre venner. De fölte seg alltid velkommen og trengte de räd og stötte var mor og far alltid tilgjengelig.  Da venner 
2 CONC av meg fra Tyskland traff mor oppe pä hytten og fortalte at de skulle kjöre innom Bergen, sa hun med et lurt glimt i öyet at de kunne jo bo hos henne hvis de hadde noe i bytte. Mine venner vekslet fort blikk og tilböd henne ä laer
2 CONC e henne ä bruke computer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Det var ogsä mange som sökte hjelp hos mor og far i tunge stunder. Tap av kjaere er ikke sä lett ä snakke om, men med mor og far fant de noen som visste hva dette var. Wollert Andreas var ogsä et tungt tema i hele livet til mor og
2 CONC  far. De bearbeidet det pä hver sin mäte, men det gjorde ogsä at de fikk et veldig sterkt bänd seg i mellom. Uansett hva som skjedde etter dette kunne ingenting mäle seg med denne opplevelsen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tiltross for tapet av min brorl fikk vi mye frihet. Vi ble oppdradd med mottoet frihet under ansvar.  Utrolig at vi fikk lov til alt vi gjorde. Vi ble sendt alene av gärde, og senere benyttet vi sjansen og reiste alene. Det var fl
2 CONC ere av oss som hadde samme önske som mor hadde hatt da hun var ung. Ut og se verden. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Men vi i laerte ogsä ä sette pris pä det som var rundt oss og pä naturen. Väre ferier bestod stort sett av ä vaere pä sjöen og eller ä gä i flellet, og hun elsket ä vaere pä hytten vär. Det var her hun dro for ä fä fred og samle k
2 CONC refter. I oktober var mor sammen med mine dötre pä hytten og vi gikk rundt Geilovannet. Mor var lett til beins og vi gikk og snakket og lo. Hun hadde fätt livslysten tilbake etter pappas bortgang  og gledet seg over livets smä gle
2 CONC der. Det er slik jeg vil huske henne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Far sa alltid de siste ärene för han döde at nä var de kommet opp i Avgangsklassen, og denne gangen var det mamma som tok  eksamen. Jeg er sikker pä at hun hadde fätt seks  i livets skole. Hun tok farvel med flagget til topps, og 
2 CONC nä  häper jeg at hun sitter händ i händ med far,  med lille Wollert mellom seg. Pappa forteller en av sine gamle vitser og mamma MÅ trekke pä smilebändet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vi kommer til ä savne deg, hvil ifred, mamma
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of Kerstin ashes was placed next to Wollert Hvide at Storetveit Kirke, the rest of her ashes were spread at a location between Ustetind and Tuva (close to Ustaoset, Norway) as per her wishes.
