1 OCCU Danish memoirist known for Grandma's Confessions (Grandmamas Bekiendelser) 
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sophie_Thalbitzer
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.kvinfo.dk/side/597/bio/932/origin/170/
2 CONT
2 CONT *
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophie Thalbitzer (née Zinn, 15 April 1774 – 27 December 1851) was a Danish memoirist known for Grandma's Confessions (Danish: Grandmamas Bekiendelser), which offers a rear first-hand account of everyday life for a child and young
2 CONC  woman in an upper-class bourgeois family in Copenhagen during the late 18th and early 19th century. She was a daughter of the wealthy merchant Johann Ludvig Zinn and grew up in the Zinn House at Kvæsthusgade 3.The building was li
2 CONC sted on the Danish registry of protected buildings and places in 1959.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophie Dorothea Zinn was born into a wealthy merchant family in Copenhagen, the daughter of Johann Ludvig Zinn and his wife.  She read many books and was particularly struck by Jean-Jacques Rousseau's Confessions.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of her father's business partners, Hinrich Ladiges, a wealthy sugar manufacturer who was 40 years older than her, proposed to her when she was 17 years old. Her father was strongly in favour of the liaison but she declined. Sh
2 CONC e later married Henry Thalbitzer (1767–1818) who was Royal Prussian consul to Helsingør. She was widowed in 1818.
2 CONT
2 CONT She wrote her memoirs after she became a widow at the age of 41. They were originally intended for their son Billy (Carl Wm. Thalbitzer) but later published under the name Grandmama's Bekiendelser. They offer a first-hand account 
2 CONC of everyday life as a child and young woman in an upper-class environment in Copenhagen during the late 18th and early.
2 CONT
2 CONT * 
